#
# Shakedown - Disk/Storage Stress Test Jobfile (Hardware Focused)
# Target: Thermal saturation, Controller stability, Bad Sector detection
#

# Stress-ng parameters
run sequential
timeout 360s
verify
metrics-brief
oom-avoid
log-brief
log-file disk.Log
yaml disk.yaml

# Stress-ng Stressors
# 1. HDD: The classic throughput stressor.
# Writes large sequential blocks. Best for generating base heat and testing sustained sequential speeds.
hdd 0
hdd-bytes 1G
hdd-write-size 64K

# 2. IO-Uring: The NVMe Killer.
# Uses the modern Linux io_uring interface to saturate high-speed NVMe queues.
# If an NVMe drive is going to drop off the bus due to heat/load, this triggers it.
io-uring 0

# 3. Seek: Random Access Hell.
# Forces the drive to jump around.
# On HDDs: Stresses the mechanical arm/motor.
# On SSDs: Stresses the internal lookup tables (FTL) and Garbage Collection.
seek 0
seek-size 512M

# 4. MMAP: Memory Mapped I/O.
# Tests the path between Disk <-> Page Cache <-> RAM.
# Excellent for catching data corruption during transfer.
mmap 0
mmap-bytes 256M

# 5. AIO: Asynchronous I/O.
# Stresses the drive's ability to handle parallel requests (Queue Depth).
aio 0
aio-requests 64

# 6. Sync-File: The Cache Buster.
# Forces the drive to flush its internal DRAM cache to permanent storage repeatedly.
# Tests the drive's power-loss protection logic and write durability.
sync-file 0
sync-file-bytes 64M
